#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81eab9b6-2c22-56c2-8b09-bb7ba88bb023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4,$ $2,$ $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383412} {:answer/id #uuid "45fdf803-5c63-5fd1-98ac-6d33e693cf05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4,$ $3,$ $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383413} {:answer/id #uuid "8b43b2a5-0f26-58bc-89b2-44f5ea0b5aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4,$ $4,$ $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383414} {:answer/id #uuid "e057365e-ebcb-5deb-8291-d5e0f828271e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3,$ $2,$ $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383415} {:answer/id #uuid "8afd4451-cbc1-5888-a4c6-5aa0879d00a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2,$ $2,$ $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e057365e-ebcb-5deb-8291-d5e0f828271e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3,$ $2,$ $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383415} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d2e9298-f7ae-583b-b56c-2427f10af7e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a2eadfc-214c-5e6d-a649-83f2e39bac57" :question/math-academy-id "q-139505" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2bf7196-b681-5ff6-bf16-24acefa04e6c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Square Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7d4ed93-75bb-5db0-9900-2362979d6c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4,\\,3,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404734} {:answer/id #uuid "aac056f1-2c7a-51fc-b3b8-c7516856527b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404735} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef4562a-20e9-5af5-80a4-ced17b367b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404736} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4157e7-a2cf-562a-af6a-fea80f5a7f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16,\\,9,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404737} {:answer/id #uuid "95d80c78-37a1-5b82-8b12-a47780191024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9,\\,4,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aac056f1-2c7a-51fc-b3b8-c7516856527b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404735} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce3dc232-fc1d-50e4-99b4-15ed1b8fa187" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9152817-159b-5a54-868d-fe52d19640cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-94981" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4-\\lambda  \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(20-4\\lambda -5\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-16)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4)-4(4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4-4) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e70dec8f-9828-5fe7-bdb7-47e6557fc889" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea50afa6-cf48-5a28-88f9-d3e965d42d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404740} {:answer/id #uuid "58501d70-837f-5aaa-b030-23424dea0d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{10}$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404741} {:answer/id #uuid "5b30f13e-19e7-5a1b-8f5a-9d9ae0821275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404742} {:answer/id #uuid "09c94f4b-6a80-5db2-b8a4-38435a2f0752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404743} {:answer/id #uuid "185ef847-122c-55fe-afa8-642c48c26e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09c94f4b-6a80-5db2-b8a4-38435a2f0752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404743} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1608723-f0ef-5251-b5a7-f10ee403c96e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a7c1d3e-ea07-5462-b92b-00ccbee66e53" :question/math-academy-id "q-94982" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184197 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8063bc9c-a10d-562b-93b1-508b4b26a674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Singular Values of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17199cb6-adea-5b61-919d-b8c404e0acce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454196} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2c651a-9fde-5d68-8dc6-db31d39c222f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d2c651a-9fde-5d68-8dc6-db31d39c222f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454197} :answer-field/id #uuid "4075aa54-0dff-530d-8aaa-0316bbe68384" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454848} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecfe78bb-58cf-5df3-9fec-25b0e56a5cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454198} {:answer/id #uuid "1cdaafa5-1a83-54e7-855b-cedee6333689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecfe78bb-58cf-5df3-9fec-25b0e56a5cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454198} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebe209e3-0b27-5e04-917c-a2a111895c0f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454849} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72750c08-7405-534f-a4d7-2b63080f7417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454200} {:answer/id #uuid "cf58434d-bb62-5004-8244-73fdeaf3e6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72750c08-7405-534f-a4d7-2b63080f7417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454200} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b11c932-8a01-5b51-9317-14a5c84895c3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fef86daf-091a-52fa-bcf0-1d2b609bb348" :question/math-academy-id "q-95281" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $6\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- **II.** The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- **III.** Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values." :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e70dec8f-9828-5fe7-bdb7-47e6557fc889" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Singular Values of a Rectangular Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d7b9b80-3654-5da0-af41-f1dd281a1ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9,\\,3,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404796} {:answer/id #uuid "adfd1028-f71d-5fb8-bf65-f6a904a4cad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404797} {:answer/id #uuid "421f5096-6ffe-58b8-95fd-205273c0d9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404798} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9e32e2-6234-54e2-a995-a9f7bc9f67fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4,\\,2,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404799} {:answer/id #uuid "b769f855-028a-56bf-bb0f-d1970e2ab6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "421f5096-6ffe-58b8-95fd-205273c0d9f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,\\,1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404798} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e48eb31-ad56-59b2-a6ab-bf3fcb90c449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de321704-9fad-5ba9-910c-dac492b8f0a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95377" :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )[(8-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4)]+2(-2)(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4)+(-4)(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +4-4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,1,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{3} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{3}}=\\sqrt{0}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
